When Michael Rupnow arrived at his Schaumburg office Tuesday, about 20 angry people were at the door. By noon, more than 100 phone messages were on his desk, more than he could handle.
Ah, the life of a township assessor in a year of a Cook County triennial property reassessment.
Rupnow is not alone. Not far away, Palatine Township Assessor Arthur Jicha also is busy, handling about 90 complaints a day this week.
Neither township official had anything to do with the cause of the taxpayers` discontent. But as local ombudsmen, the township assessors are ready to help constituents find a basis for a formal complaint against what Rupnow decries as ''the inefficiency of the county assessor.''
The last of the year`s reassessment notices either have been delivered or are about to be placed in the mail. In the last few days, owners of property in Schaumburg, Palatine and Northfield Townships received their reassessments. And those owning property in Wheeling and Maine Townships can expect theirs in the next few days.
This year`s reassessment involved 391,000 parcels of real estate in 13 townships of north and northwest suburban Cook County, according to David Druker, a spokesman for County Assessor Thomas Hynes.
Notices began going out last June. Since then, the county assessor has received thousands of challenges to the new assessments in the form of formal complaints.
Rupnow said the entire assessment system needs reform.
''There are so many whistles and bells in our system, the average property owner can`t understand it,'' he said. ''And it`s not just residential owners. Commercial property owners also are crying bloody murder.''
Druker took exception to Rupnow`s remarks about the inefficiencies of the county assessor, saying Hynes has ''brought many reforms to the office.''
As an example, he said, Hynes` staff is also ready and eager to assist property owners in filing out their complaints. To accommodate them, Druker said, the assessor`s office is open from 9 a.m. to noon Saturdays in the Circuit Court buildings of Rolling Meadows, Skokie and Maywood as well as the County Building in downtown Chicago.
The offices are open from 8:45 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. weekdays, he said.
Part of the county assessor`s taxpayer service is called Project Reachout, where a hearing officer speaks with individual homeowners and helps them complete a complaint form.
But it must be done within the notice deadline, 30 days after the property owner gets his reassessment, Druker said.
After that, the property owner still has time, before tax bills arrive in the mail, to appeal their reassessment directly to the Cook County Board of
