The last obstacles a manager wants to deal with heading into the late stages of the season are an unstable top portion of the batting order and a shaky pitching rotation.
However, that's just what Norfolk Tides skipper John Gibbons is facing.
After the New York Mets opted to trade Tides outfielder Jason Tyner and pitcher Paul Wilson to Tampa Bay on Friday, Norfolk was left without the International League's most potent leadoff hitter and one of the league's hottest starting pitchers. The Mets received outfielder Bubba Trammell and relief pitcher Rick White from the Devil Rays.
Tyner and Wilson were only two of five former Tides shipped off to new locations Friday. In an effort to solidify its shortstop position defensively going into the final months of the season, New York traded pitchers Pat Gorman and Lesli Brea, catcher-outfielder Mike Kinkade and utility player Melvin Mora to Baltimore in return for shortstop Mike Bordick. Brea, Kinkade and Mora have all spent various amounts of time with the Tides.
So, Gibbons is saddled with the duty of tinkering with the lineup to squeeze runs out of it and trying to find another dependable starting pitcher. At least the trades didn't catch him by complete surprise. Whenever it gets close to the July 31 trade deadline, most minor-league squads brace themselves for the inevitable roster shakeup that comes with it.
"We kind of anticipated something and it finally happened," Gibbons said. "We expected they were going to try to get a shortstop, which meant big things to Mora. I'm sure Tampa Bay wanted (Wilson) pretty bad, and they tracked him."
There has been plenty to watch from the resurgent Wilson in recent weeks. Though he has earned just one win in his last three starts, the 27-year-old right-hander has compiled an astounding ERA of 0.43 and allowed 13 hits and one run in 21 innings over that span. He finished 5-5 with a 4.23 ERA this year with the Tides.
Tyner left the Tides with a.321 batting average to go along with his league-leading 33 steals.
The Tides got immediate offensive assistance from the Mets in the form of infielder Mark Johnson and infielder Jorge Toca, who was actually promoted to New York on Friday but returned Saturday following the trades. Norfolk should also receive a pitching arm, most likely from Class AA Binghamton (N.Y.), in the near future.
